{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-n-equals-1-vacuous-magic","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:n-equals-1-vacuous-magic","kind":"proposition","title":"Vacuous primality at $n = 1$","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-algebraic-primality"],"tags":["magic-numbers","primality","base-case","vacuous-truth","proved"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.Interactions.AlgebraicPrimality.one_isAlgebraicallyPrime","text":"n-equals-1-vacuous-magic.md","leanSource":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/nuclear-interactions/n-equals-1-vacuous-magic.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"prop:n-equals-1-vacuous-magic\"></a><p><em>For every substrate <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q > 1</code>, the integer <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n = 1</code> is algebraically prime. Equivalently, the canonical state <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\psi_1(q)</code> is not in the closed span of any non-trivial tensor product of canonical states indexed by a decomposition <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1 = n_1 + \\cdots + n_k</code> with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">k \\geq 2</code> and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n_i \\geq 1</code>. No such decomposition exists.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Archimedean specialisation.</strong> What follows sits on the archimedean side of the substrate wall.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/nuclear-interactions/n-equals-1-vacuous-magic.md","heading":"Proposition","number":"11.2.18"}
